= Research Question

Prince Edward Island's changing coastline raises questions about where erosion persists and how short periods of change compare with longer records.

= My Contribution

I organized CHRIS shoreline and coastal-protection data, made QGIS maps, and summarized existing shoreline change rates by coastal setting. With guidance from colleagues, I also analyzed historical hotspot persistence and changes near coastal structures at Naufrage, and presented these analyses in group meetings.

= Results and Output

A jointly authored interim report with Julia Pulawski records the mapping and initial analysis. The Naufrage analysis used 1,052 common transects in 51 spatial groups. Historical hotspots were defined from 1968–2010 rates and recent hotspots from 2020–2022 rates; three of the eleven recent hotspots overlapped with historical hotspots.

For each transect, I compared the annualized southward displacement rate in 2020–2022 with its rate in 2010–2020. The regional median of the within-group median differences was 0.926 m/yr. The 95% spatial-block bootstrap interval included zero when using 10 km blocks. Positive displacement is southward; interpreting it as erosion, a storm effect, or an effect of coastal protection requires further information.
